1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:00,701
Decilo.

2
00:00:00,701 --> 00:00:05,112
Live captions so no idea gets left out.

3
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:10,388
At a conference, language should never leave
anyone out.

4
00:00:10,388 --> 00:00:15,096
Decilo transcribes English and Spanish, and
translates English into Spanish.

5
00:00:16,150 --> 00:00:20,499
Paste a link, choose the language and share
the tab audio.

6
00:00:20,499 --> 00:00:26,033
Whisper transcribes on your machine; when
the audio is in English, Gemma translates it

7
00:00:26,033 --> 00:00:26,824
into Spanish.

8
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:33,617
For cloud processing, choose Gemini.

9
00:00:33,617 --> 00:00:39,045
Audio is transcribed live, and translation
appears incrementally while the history

10
00:00:39,045 --> 00:00:41,512
keeps track of the talk.

11
00:00:46,150 --> 00:00:51,587
Two different talks are being translated at
the same time in the cloud.

12
00:00:51,587 --> 00:00:54,932
Each session keeps its own audio and
captions.

13
00:00:54,932 --> 00:00:58,696
More viewers can follow a session without
duplicating inference.

14
00:01:10,150 --> 00:01:15,458
We also tested a Spanish talk, both locally
and in the cloud.

15
00:01:15,458 --> 00:01:18,112
Here, we show the original transcription.

16
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:27,029
The browser sends audio to the Python
backend.

17
00:01:27,029 --> 00:01:32,364
It selects the local engine or Gemini,
coordinates sessions and distributes

18
00:01:32,364 --> 00:01:33,818
captions over WebSocket.

19
00:01:33,818 --> 00:01:36,728
Processing is separate from the audience.

20
00:01:45,150 --> 00:01:49,111
Decilo is open source under the Apache 2.0
license.

21
00:01:49,111 --> 00:01:52,632
The code and setup instructions are on
GitHub.
